{"study_accession":"PRJNA1524248","secondary_study_accession":"SRP733894","title":"Transcriptomic and phenotypic analyses reveal time-qualified patterns featuring Chimeric Antigen Receptor-T cells (CAR-T) dynamics","description":"Immune function follows circadian rhythms, and time-of-day of infusion may influence the efficacy of Chimeric Antigen Receptor T-cell (CAR-T) therapy. To investigate the transcriptional and phenotypic basis of this phenomenon, CD7-CAR T cells and empty vector (EV) controls were synchronized by serum shock and profiled by RNA sequencing across a 24-hour time-course (0, 6, 12, 18, 24 h), alongside cosinor analysis of cytotoxicity, cytokine secretion, exhaustion markers, and immunophenotyping. CD7-CAR T cells showed a more coordinated core clock gene network than EV controls, with hallmark immune/proliferative pathways peaking in a late window (16–20 h) and greater-amplitude oscillations in cytotoxicity and cytokine secretion. Circadian phase inferred in an independent T-ALL cohort (n = 1,334) using CYCLOPS was concordant with the in vitro model, and event-free survival was significantly worse in the fourth phase quartile (p = 0.011). These findings indicate that CD7-CAR T cells retain a partially reorganized circadian oscillator governing their effector programs, supporting infusion timing as a translatable strategy for CAR-T therapy.","pubmed":"","geo_series":"","n_samples":20,"samples":[{"sample_accession":"SAMN62958793","study_accession":"PRJNA1524248","blood_class":"plasma_serum_cfrna","class_from":"sample","title":"CAR-T, CD7, T24h, rep2","description":"CAR-T, CD7, T24h, rep2","sex":"","geo_sample":"GSM10031197","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":2,"first_public":"2026-09-08","attributes":[["source_name","Pheripheral Blood"],["tissue","Pheripheral Blood"],["cell_type","Pan-T cells"],["genotype","CD7 Knockdown and transduced with a with a lentiviral vector encoding a second-generation CAR targeting CD7"],["treatment","Serum Schock Synchr